r = await lrn_llm.ping()
print(f"✅ LLM reachable: {r['ok']}")
print(f"Model: {r['model']}")

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 2 — The Support Ticket Triage Problem
#
# You work as a support-ops engineer at a SaaS company. Raw customer emails flood in daily. You need to:
# - Extract key information (urgency, category, issue summary)
# - Assign a priority level (critical, high, medium, low)
# - Route to the right team (billing, technical, general)
#
# **The challenge:** a vague prompt produces inconsistent, hard-to-parse triage data. An engineered prompt with examples produces clean, uniform output.
#
# Let's see the difference.

# %% [markdown]
# ### Sample Support Ticket
#
# Here's a real customer email we'll triage twice — once with a vague prompt, once with an engineered few-shot prompt.

# %%
sample_ticket = """Subject: App keeps crashing on login

Hi,

Every time I try to log in to the mobile app, it crashes after 2-3 seconds. 
I've tried clearing cache, restarting my phone, and even reinstalling the app, 
but nothing works. This is blocking me from accessing my project data. 
I need this fixed ASAP as I have a presentation tomorrow.

Thanks,
Sarah"""

print("=== TICKET TO TRIAGE ===")
print(sample_ticket)

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 3 — VAGUE Prompt (Baseline)
#
# Why vague prompts fail: no examples, no output format, no constraints. The model has to guess what "triage" means.

# %%
vague_prompt = """You are a support triage agent. Look at this customer email and triage it.

Email:
{}

Provide your triage analysis.""".format(sample_ticket)

print("=== VAGUE PROMPT ===")
print(vague_prompt)
print("\n... sending to LLM ...\n")

vague_response = await lrn_llm.call(
    [{"role": "user", "content": vague_prompt}],
    system="You are a support triage agent.",
    max_tokens=300
)
vague_output = lrn_llm.text(vague_response)
print("=== VAGUE OUTPUT ===")
print(vague_output)

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 4 — ENGINEERED Prompt (Few-Shot Pattern)
#
# Now we use the few-shot pattern from the lesson:
# - Specific role ("support triage specialist at a B2B SaaS company")
# - Clear output format (structured fields: priority, category, summary, urgency)
# - 2 examples showing the exact format we want
# - Explicit constraints (no markdown, no preamble, JSON output)

# %%
engineered_prompt = """You are a support triage specialist at a B2B SaaS company. 
Your job is to convert raw customer emails into structured triage records.

Here are examples of correct triage output:

=== EXAMPLE 1 ===
Customer email: "I can't pay my invoice. The payment page keeps erroring out after I enter my card. Very urgent!"

Triage output:
{{"priority": "critical", "category": "billing", "urgency_hours": 1, "summary": "Payment page fails when processing card entry; customer cannot complete purchase"}}

=== EXAMPLE 2 ===
Customer email: "The dashboard seems slow today. Just wanted to give you a heads up."

Triage output:
{{"priority": "low", "category": "technical", "urgency_hours": 24, "summary": "Dashboard performance degradation reported; no blockers mentioned"}}

=== NOW TRIAGE THIS EMAIL ===

{}

Output ONLY a valid JSON object with keys: priority (critical|high|medium|low), category (billing|technical|general|feature_request), urgency_hours (integer), summary (string under 30 words). No preamble, no explanation, just the JSON.""".format(sample_ticket)

print("=== ENGINEERED PROMPT ===")
print(engineered_prompt[:500] + "...\n")
print("... sending to LLM ...\n")

engineered_response = await lrn_llm.call(
    [{"role": "user", "content": engineered_prompt}],
    system="You are a support triage specialist. Output valid JSON only.",
    max_tokens=200
)
engineered_output = lrn_llm.text(engineered_response)
print("=== ENGINEERED OUTPUT ===")
print(engineered_output)

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 5 — Compare the Outputs
#
# The vague prompt produces prose. The engineered prompt produces structured JSON.
#
# Let's parse both and see what we can extract:

# %%
print("=== COMPARISON ===")
print("\n[VAGUE PROMPT]")
print("Output style: Free-form prose")
print(f"Length: {len(vague_output)} chars")
print(f"Parseable JSON: No")
print(f"Programmatic use: ❌ Requires regex/NLP to extract data")

print("\n[ENGINEERED PROMPT (few-shot)]")
print("Output style: Structured JSON")
print(f"Length: {len(engineered_output)} chars")
try:
    parsed = json.loads(engineered_output.strip())
    print(f"Parseable JSON: ✅ Yes")
    print(f"Keys extracted: {list(parsed.keys())}")
    print(f"Programmatic use: ✅ Direct access to fields")
    print(f"Priority: {parsed.get('priority')}")
    print(f"Category: {parsed.get('category')}")
    print(f"Urgency (hours): {parsed.get('urgency_hours')}")
except json.JSONDecodeError:
    print(f"Parseable JSON: ❌ Failed to parse")
    print(f"First 200 chars: {engineered_output[:200]}")

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 6 — Why This Works: The Prompt Patterns in Action
#
# The engineered prompt combined three patterns from the lesson:
#
# **1. Persona Pattern**: "You are a support triage specialist at a B2B SaaS company."  
# → Activates the correct distribution of expert triage language in the model's weights.
#
# **2. Few-Shot Pattern**: Two labeled input/output examples  
# → Shows the exact format, field names, and reasoning the model should replicate.
#
# **3. Output Format Control**: "Output ONLY a valid JSON object... No preamble..."  
# → Constrains the output space to exactly what the system needs.
#
# Each pattern narrows the probability distribution the model samples from. Narrower distribution → higher quality and consistency.

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 7 — Build a Test Harness
#
# Now let's run the same two tickets through both prompts to see the pattern hold.

# %%
# A dataset of support tickets to test
test_tickets = [
    ("urgent_billing", "My subscription renewed but I was charged TWICE. Please refund immediately. This is unacceptable."),
    ("low_priority_feature", "Would be cool if the dashboard had a dark mode. Not urgent."),
]

async def triage_vague(ticket_text):
    prompt = f"You are a support triage agent. Triage this customer email:\n\n{ticket_text}"
    r = await lrn_llm.call(
        [{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
        system="You are a support triage agent.",
        max_tokens=250
    )
    return lrn_llm.text(r)

async def triage_engineered(ticket_text):
    prompt = f"""You are a support triage specialist at a B2B SaaS company.

Examples of correct output:
Email: "Can't access billing portal." → {{"priority": "high", "category": "billing", "urgency_hours": 4, "summary": "Billing portal access blocked"}}
Email: "App is slow today." → {{"priority": "low", "category": "technical", "urgency_hours": 24, "summary": "Performance issue reported"}}

Triage this email: {ticket_text}

Output ONLY valid JSON with keys: priority, category, urgency_hours, summary. No other text."""
    r = await lrn_llm.call(
        [{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
        system="You are a support triage specialist. Output JSON only.",
        max_tokens=150
    )
    return lrn_llm.text(r)

print("Testing two tickets with both prompt approaches...\n")

for label, ticket in test_tickets:
    print(f"\n{'='*60}")
    print(f"Ticket: {label}")
    print(f"Content: {ticket[:60]}...")
    print(f"{'='*60}")
    
    vague_result = await triage_vague(ticket)
    print(f"\n[VAGUE] {vague_result[:150]}...")
    
    engineered_result = await triage_engineered(ticket)
    print(f"\n[ENGINEERED] {engineered_result}")
    try:
        parsed = json.loads(engineered_result.strip())
        print(f"   ✅ Valid JSON: {list(parsed.keys())}")
    except:
        print(f"   ❌ Invalid JSON")

# %% [markdown]
# ## Step 8 — Key Takeaway: Specificity Wins
#
# Compare what we gave the model:
#
# | Dimension | Vague Prompt | Engineered Prompt |
# |-----------|---|---|
# | Role | Generic "triage agent" | Specific role + company context |
# | Format | Implicit, prose expected | Explicit JSON schema |
# | Examples | None | 2 labeled examples |
# | Constraints | Vague ("triage it") | Specific (JSON keys, no preamble) |
# | Output | Free-form, variable | Uniform, parseable |
# | Integration | Manual parsing needed | Direct programmatic access |
#
# The engineered prompt is NOT longer. It is **more specific**. Specificity is the entire craft of prompt engineering.

